In C++, vector empty() is a built-in method used to check whether the given vector is empty or not. In this article, we will learn about vector empty() method in C++.Let’s take a look at an example that illustrates the vector empty() method:
            C++

    #include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> v = {11, 23, 45, 9};

    // Check if the vector is empty
    if (v.empty()) {
        cout << "Empty";
    } else {
        cout << "Not empty";
    }

    return 0;
}

